00:00:58.992 --> 00:01:00.700
JASON SCOTT: Yes, so
the startup program.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.370
I would say there are three big
ways that we support startups.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.010
The first is access.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.410
So as you know,
access to product,

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.380
whether those be Cloud
products or Firebase,

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:13.540
are Maps APIs if they're
doing cool things with maps.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:18.490
Or even access to technical
resources as well.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:20.540
So we do a lot of
technical office hours

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.230
and one-on-one architecture
reviews, things like that,

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:24.010
for startups.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:25.551
But I would one of
the biggest things

00:01:25.551 --> 00:01:27.260
that we provide is
just the community.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.551
So we love connecting startups
that are building things

00:01:29.551 --> 00:01:32.390
on GCP, and connecting them
to the outside community.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:35.140
So here on the
West Coast, I love

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.260
connecting startups to partners
that we have in the ecosystem.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.242
So accelerators
and VCs, as well as

00:01:41.242 --> 00:01:43.450
just Googlers who are really
passionate about working

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:44.089
with startups.

00:01:44.089 --> 00:01:45.130
NICK ORLOVE: That's cool.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:49.090
It's basically all about who you
know sometimes in the startup

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:52.000
world, so if Google is able
to help me get connected,

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.635
or help my startup get connected
with Googlers, and partners,

00:01:54.635 --> 00:01:56.510
and VCs, and all that,
that's really awesome.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:02:01.120
So why should a startup
kind of consider

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.180
going with maybe the GCP
startup program, as opposed

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:08.123
to jumping in on their own
without the GCP program

00:02:08.123 --> 00:02:09.039
or anything like that?

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:11.470
JASON SCOTT: Yeah,
fundamentally, I

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.150
would say Googlers
just love startups.

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:14.885
And Google, I think
fundamentally also,

00:02:14.885 --> 00:02:15.510
loves startups.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.426
And I think whether it
be the right commitment

00:02:17.426 --> 00:02:19.450
to Open Source, and you
see that with Kubernetes

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.570
and with TensorFlow, but
also through just a lot

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:24.340
of our activities
that we do as a whole.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:26.050
We really believe
in the one Google.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:27.797
And I am always
pleasantly surprised

00:02:27.797 --> 00:02:30.130
when I'm able to do an event,
and I not just help people

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.213
at Cloud, but have people
come in from other teams

00:02:32.213 --> 00:02:33.880
across Google to also
support startups.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.550
And I think Google is one
of those few places where

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.617
you can do that, where you
actually have people that

00:02:39.617 --> 00:02:42.230
are helping startups with
all of their infrastructure

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:45.730
and all their products
throughout their business model

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.500
and their other
stages of growth.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:48.850
NICK ORLOVE: That's true.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.860
Google Cloud is,
obviously, one aspect

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:52.690
