00:07:54.766 --> 00:07:55.830
what LiftIgniter does?

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.163
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: Absolutely.

00:07:57.163 --> 00:07:59.450
Thanks for having me here, Nick.

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:01.530
It's a pleasure to be here.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:04.080
So LiftIgniter is
a machine learning

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.080
personalization company.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:08.310
And the best way
to think about that

00:08:08.310 --> 00:08:11.130
is, you think of a
site like YouTube,

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.830
where every time
you watch a video,

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:17.190
a few videos are picked
automatically for you

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:18.660
to watch next.

00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:21.630
And most of you who
spend time on YouTube

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.150
know how addicting it is.

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:27.060
But what you may not
realize is behind the scenes

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.330
there is this
enormously powerful

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:35.220
artificial intelligence software
that is picking videos for you

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:37.260
from a pool of
billions of videos.

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:39.690
And it's doing it
for every single user

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:41.400
that comes to this site.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:43.419
It's an enormously
difficult problem.

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:46.830
But once we do it right, if
you can pick the right video

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:49.770
that a user is interested
in watching next,

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:52.480
you will create that
addictive experience,

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:55.830
which means end users have a
much more satisfying experience

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:57.060
on YouTube.

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:00.700
And it also means that they will
spend less time on other sites.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:03.480
So it's a critical thing for
the website owners to have,

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:08.970
and it's an important component
of the user experience.

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:12.430
And the only way to make
this possible at scale

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:16.350
in this attention economy
is to have intelligence

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:19.020
behind the scenes, which is
only possible through machine

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:22.530
learning and more
recent AI techniques.

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:26.190
So that is what
LiftIgniter tries

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:29.640
to bring to the rest of
the world, which may not

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:35.280
be able to hire the
same kind of engineers

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.130
as Google or Facebook
can, or build

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:40.122
the kind of infrastructure
that they can.

00:09:40.122 --> 00:09:42.080
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah, that's
an interesting point.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.340
So essentially, you're
taking the concept

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:46.380
behind YouTube
recommendations and all that,

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.050
and then applying them to,
really, all of your customers

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:50.310
out there.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:51.660
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: Exactly.

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:54.870
The concept of personalization
has been around

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:56.700
since the start of the internet.

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:59.640
You had personalization
companies even in '99.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.340
What has truly changed
is-- and we always

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:05.700
knew that in order to
do personalization,

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.810
you need to build
intelligence at scale.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:13.710
And what has changed recently
is massive breakthroughs

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.780
in machine learning,
massive breakthroughs

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:17.820
in cloud computing.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:20.540
The presence of cloud
computing solutions

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:24.210
and amazing
infrastructure has made

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:27.000
it possible for startups
like us to actually build

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:30.680
these solutions and make it
available at a reasonable price

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:32.530
to the rest of the world.

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:37.470
And this is why the genesis
of such solutions happened

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.250
in companies like
Google and Facebook,

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:41.010
which had the absolute
state-of-the-art

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:43.470
in infrastructure and some
of the best engineers,

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.100
massive resources.

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.270
But now it's come to a point,
with these waves of advances

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:50.880
in machine learning and
cloud computing, that it

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:53.580
can be made available to the
rest of the world via startups

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:54.570
like LiftIgniter.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:55.510
NICK ORLOVE: That's
really awesome.

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:57.000
And it's so exciting
that you guys

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:59.730
are pretty much on the
breaking edge of doing this

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:01.840
and it's really thanks
to machine learning.

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:04.740
So I guess some of
the basic questions

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:08.970
is, how or when did
you found LiftIgniter,

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:12.310
and why did you get into the
space in the first place?

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:14.520
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE:
Yeah, LiftIgniter actually

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:17.620
has an interesting
genesis story.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:21.780
So I was part of the
Google research team

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:25.350
that built out several of the
major personalization systems

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:28.360
at Google, including
that of YouTube.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:33.140
So I got to see firsthand
what a massive impact

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:36.900
in a true real-time
machine learning at scale

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:40.450
can have on customer
satisfaction,

00:11:40.450 --> 00:11:44.310
on business metrics
for the website owners.

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:46.350
And so I felt like
this technology

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:51.637
had to be made available
at a reasonable price point

00:11:51.637 --> 00:11:52.720
for the rest of the world.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:59.580
And so this was about
four years ago, and so

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:00.894
basically that was the story.

00:12:00.894 --> 00:12:03.060
That was the inspiration--
some of the breakthroughs

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:05.040
that happened in
companies inside Google

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.512
and saw similar breakthroughs
happening at Facebook.

00:12:07.512 --> 00:12:08.970
We felt like this
needed to be made

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:11.054
available to the
rest of the world.

00:12:11.054 --> 00:12:12.720
NICK ORLOVE: And then
from a high level,

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.380
how exactly does it work?

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:18.230
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: So there's
a lot of things going on

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:21.470
behind the scenes,
but what we do

00:12:21.470 --> 00:12:24.130
is we look at two kinds of data.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:30.500
We look at user activities on
the site very, very carefully.

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:33.380
And then we look at
the kind of signal

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:37.460
that you can extract out
of the content itself,

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:40.460
through NLP, through
image features extraction,

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:43.570
through video analysis.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:45.410
And taking all this
data-- this data

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.732
becomes very large very quickly.

00:12:47.732 --> 00:12:48.690
NICK ORLOVE: Of course.

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:52.430
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: And
what we need to do with this

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:55.530
data is actually
even more difficult.

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:58.220
So there are three things
that we need to do.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:00.560
We need to do sophisticated
modeling in order

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:05.450
to be able to guess what a
complex creature like a human

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:07.730
being might want next.

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:13.190
We need to do this modeling at
scale, because as we discussed,

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:15.290
the data blows up really fast.

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:16.910
And finally, the
third piece is we

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:20.300
need to do it really, really
fast in the order of 100

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:23.990
milliseconds per visit.

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:28.310
And these three things,
scalability, complex modeling,

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:32.090
and real time, they don't
really go well with each other

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:33.890
unless you have
built fantastic--

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:37.130
you have access to fantastic
infrastructure, fantastic

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:39.210
mathematical algorithms.

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:41.690
So we kind of put
all that together.

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:43.910
And what ends up
happening is you

00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:47.750
have a truly dynamic
experience on the website.

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:50.840
So when a user comes,
like a site like YouTube,

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:53.830
they might be showing
sports on the top row.

00:13:53.830 --> 00:13:56.180
But you might be interested
in music right now.

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:59.652
And within a few clicks, the
algorithms have figured out

00:13:59.652 --> 00:14:00.860
you're in the mood for music.

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:02.443
So when you go back
to your home page,

00:14:02.443 --> 00:14:04.250
the first row is
now showing music.

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:07.670
So that kind of intelligence
that can dynamically

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:10.180
personalize every element,
it's possible only

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:12.410
when you combine really
sophisticated modeling,

00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:15.494
using user behavior and
content at scale in real time.

00:14:15.494 --> 00:14:16.910
NICK ORLOVE: I'm
always fascinated

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:19.070
with machine learning
use cases because it

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:21.970
seems like machine learning
sometimes can solve everything.

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:25.010
Obviously it can't, so I just
want to hear your opinion.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:28.430
What, in your application,
has machine learning

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:29.464
worked really well with?

00:14:29.464 --> 00:14:31.880
And then maybe what are some
of the challenges that you've

00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:34.338
had with machine learning,
where machine learning might not

00:14:34.338 --> 00:14:35.270
be the best use case?

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:36.603
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: Absolutely.

00:14:36.603 --> 00:14:42.530
So machine learning-- if you
can focus and narrow down

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:45.990
a use case and gather
enough data for it,

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:49.550
machine learning can
be a great solution.

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:53.120
And so it's funny.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:58.440
Machine learning
has so much buzz

00:14:58.440 --> 00:14:59.630
around machine learning now.

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:03.920
But there are-- and it
can infuse and improves

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:07.260
almost every product.

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:09.930
It can be infused
in every product,

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:17.820
but it has to fit in
natural grooves nicely.

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:23.460
So for instance, there aren't--

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:31.140
machine learning can't suddenly
predict that a certain dress

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:35.460
someone wore on
an Oscar night is

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:40.170
going to resonate with a lot
of users, which a marketer

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:43.110
or someone who has
domain expertise

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:45.690
might have a lot
of intuition for.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.894
So it's imperative that
machine learning algorithms,

00:15:48.894 --> 00:15:50.685
which can crunch a lot
of data really fast,

00:15:50.685 --> 00:15:55.380
do complicated things, be
paired with human intelligence,

00:15:55.380 --> 00:16:00.240
which can make these leaps
through vast more experience

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:04.200
and more sophisticated,
cognitive models that

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:06.720
algorithms cannot do, typically.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:09.510
So we are really, I
would say, scratching

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:14.160
the beginnings of where we
are from a sophistication

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:15.310
perspective.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:19.590
And so it still needs to be a
big partnership between humans

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:22.812
and machine learning to
be truly driving value.

00:16:22.812 --> 00:16:25.020
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah, that's
a really interesting point.

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.660
I find that machine
learning can be a little bit

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:29.820
challenging to get
into, specifically

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:31.140
for some of those reasons.

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:33.360
And it's awesome
that Google is kind

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:36.960
of releasing more and more
products like AutoML and stuff

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:40.140
like that, that can kind of
meet the user halfway, and not

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:44.337
just provide an ML API,
but allow them to put some

00:16:44.337 --> 00:16:46.920
of their secret sauce, some of
the customization on top of it.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:50.577
But you're absolutely hit on a
correct point in that, in order

00:16:50.577 --> 00:16:53.160
to do machine learning, you have
to have a whole bunch of data

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:54.474
in order to train the model.

00:16:54.474 --> 00:16:55.890
But not only do
you need the data,

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:58.620
but you need to be able
to have data that's clean,

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:00.150
that's been sanitized.

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:05.490
So some of those processes
have to be aided by people,

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:06.960
by ETO processes.

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:07.910
Stuff like that.

00:17:07.910 --> 00:17:10.160
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: Yeah,
it's actually a lot of work

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:14.130
to get the integration right
because algorithms are very

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:16.980
sensitive to feedback data.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:20.220
A lot of things have to fall
into place before it can work.

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:22.170
When it does work,
it works like magic.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:26.040
But we're still, as I said,
just scratching the surface.

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:31.290
There are lots more things
that can't be done by machine

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:33.132
learning than can be done.

00:17:33.132 --> 00:17:35.340
NICK ORLOVE: And is there
a success story that you're

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:36.427
able to share with us?

00:17:36.427 --> 00:17:38.010
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:43.410
This is a very
common example of how

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:46.620
we are used by our customers.

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:51.720
So this is a site which has a
lot of high-end fashion items,

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:55.890
and we are personalizing
a grid of products

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:57.660
that are shown to a user.

00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:00.240
This grid is fully responsive.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.850
As the user navigates
through the site,

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:04.860
as they click on
different items,

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:08.890
within a sub-second-- within
actually 100 milliseconds--

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.700
when they revisit the
grid after navigating

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:13.350
the site for a
little bit of time,

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.200
it has completely changed
to reflect the kind of mood

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.030
they might be in--
the kind of references

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:21.640
that the algorithm has
inferred the user might be in.

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:24.750
When you do that, you
see the kind of results

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:28.020
that you see here are
improvements and user

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:32.190
engagement, the time
they spend site,

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:33.850
dollars they spend on the site.

00:18:33.850 --> 00:18:35.350
Because they're
finding the products

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:37.940
they want by double digit
percentage [INAUDIBLE],,

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:38.642
which are huge.

00:18:38.642 --> 00:18:41.100
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah, and I like
your example of, essentially,

00:18:41.100 --> 00:18:42.480
user initially goes
to the site, and they

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.550
see all sorts of different
items-- dresses, shirts,

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:46.716
purses, all that-- but then
when they start clicking

00:18:46.716 --> 00:18:48.570
on some purses, you
can kind of infer

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:50.940
that the user is maybe
more searching for purses

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:52.750
rather than all their
other clothing items.

00:18:52.750 --> 00:18:53.790
So that makes sense.

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:54.900
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE:
Yeah, and it's

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:56.310
important to do
this in real time

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:58.770
because you never really
know who the user is,

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:00.140
even if they have logged in.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:03.257
Which, by the way, 90% of
the users are not logged in.

00:19:03.257 --> 00:19:05.590
And even if they're logged
in, it might be their spouse,

00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:07.160
or it might be
their kids using it.

00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:12.360
So their mood right now cannot
be informed by two-years-old

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:13.840
worth of purchasing data.

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:16.410
So it has to react in real time.

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:19.410
The best signal
for what a user is

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:21.270
going to do in the
next five seconds

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:23.580
is given by what they did
in the last five seconds.

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:25.767
And you have to build a
truly real-time machine

00:19:25.767 --> 00:19:26.850
learning solution to that.

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:28.664
NICK ORLOVE: That makes sense.

00:19:28.664 --> 00:19:31.080
And are you able to walk through
some of your architecture

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:31.630
with us?

00:19:31.630 --> 00:19:33.213
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:19:33.213 --> 00:19:38.320
And just to give you a sense
of the scale we are at,

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:42.060
which necessitates pretty
sophisticated architecture,

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:44.910
we're processing
tens of billions

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:48.120
of user activities
per month, which is--

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:49.576
NICK ORLOVE: No small feat.

00:19:49.576 --> 00:19:50.700
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: It is.

00:19:50.700 --> 00:19:54.450
We used to joke at some
point we were processing

00:19:54.450 --> 00:20:00.100
more than a billion page views
per engineer in our company.

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:05.310
So things that were absolutely
not possible 10 years ago--

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:07.780
so we're definitely leveraging
all the latest technology

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:09.160
in a very critical way.

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:15.450
But we process tens of
thousands of events per second.

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:20.500
We score millions of
content per second

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:25.790
to figure out the
right match for a user.

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:28.660
We have billions of
signals that we analyze.

00:20:28.660 --> 00:20:32.830
And all of this requires
a really sophisticated

00:20:32.830 --> 00:20:37.985
architecture, which I
can describe in a second.

00:20:37.985 --> 00:20:39.860
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah,
let's do that.

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:43.270
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: So
we're on GCP, obviously.

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:50.120
And we use a variety of
pieces of their technology.

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:54.250
This is a highly simplified
diagram, just so that--

00:20:54.250 --> 00:20:55.292
NICK ORLOVE: More arrows.

00:20:55.292 --> 00:20:57.583
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: Yeah,
there are a lot more pieces,

00:20:57.583 --> 00:20:58.570
a lot more arrows.

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:02.950
But this already shows how we
critically use real pieces.

00:21:02.950 --> 00:21:06.730
So we have-- and as I've said,
probably the most common word

00:21:06.730 --> 00:21:08.300
I've used is real time.

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:11.860
So we have lots of
pieces to make as much

00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:13.990
of the processing in
real time as possible.

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:16.960
So, for instance,
we have a cluster--

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:20.200
an autoscaling cluster of
front-end computing instances,

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:22.840
which receive a mass--

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:24.550
this fire hose of events.

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:28.930
Then we process them through
the Google Cloud Pub/Sub message

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:33.350
queue, and then behind the
consumers of those message

00:21:33.350 --> 00:21:36.400
queue are these real-time
jobs that are also

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:37.930
on autoscaling clusters.

00:21:37.930 --> 00:21:40.480
One thing we use here
is-- to keep costs low,

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:42.310
which is critical
for startups-- is

00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:51.040
we use preemptible instances
which are much more cheap.

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:56.650
Because our real-time jobs, they
require less strict guarantees

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:58.550
because it's
statistical systems.

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:00.370
If there is a little
bit of loss of data,

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:02.370
that's fine for the machine
learning algorithms.

00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:06.970
So we can keep flexible,
preemptible cluster running.

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:09.327
All of that data goes
into our data store,

00:22:09.327 --> 00:22:11.410
which is a core piece of
our infrastructure, which

00:22:11.410 --> 00:22:15.280
supports hundreds of thousands
of rewrites per second.

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:18.850
and that is built on a cluster
of Google computing instances.

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:20.830
We use a variety
of cloud storage.

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:22.870
We use BigQuery-- very critical.

00:22:22.870 --> 00:22:25.900
I personally think BigQuery is
a fantastic piece of software.

00:22:25.900 --> 00:22:30.996
It has eliminated several
quarters worth of development

00:22:30.996 --> 00:22:32.620
that you would have
had to do otherwise

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:35.300
in order to create
real-time analytics.

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:39.070
And it's a great tool for
non-technical customers,

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:40.750
or non-technical
members of our company

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:42.860
to analyze data as well.

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:44.740
And finally, from the
machine learning side,

00:22:44.740 --> 00:22:47.110
we built a lot of that
machine learning in-house,

00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:51.070
but we also use some of
some deep learning APIs,

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:54.790
like for vision and NLP,
for which Google has

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:58.360
the state-of-the-art, the
best APIs around at a very

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.314
reasonable cost.

00:23:00.314 --> 00:23:02.230
NICK ORLOVE: So I'm just
a little bit curious.

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:03.938
Looks like a lot of
your machine learning

00:23:03.938 --> 00:23:06.771
happens in GC, rather than
maybe the Cloud Machine Learning

00:23:06.771 --> 00:23:07.270
Engine.

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:10.600
So would you mind just adding
some context of why you

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:12.350
went with one versus the other?

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:16.400
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: Yeah,
some of it is historical.

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:19.630
As we'll cover, we
weren't originally on GCP.

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:21.700
In fact, we started
before a Google Cloud

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:25.520
was a big enterprise.

00:23:25.520 --> 00:23:28.600
So we had to build a lot of
our own machine learning.

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:33.370
Some of it is because we do
very custom machine learning

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:36.460
solutions, which are not
available as free tools.

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:40.090
But that said, we use
some ready-made APIs,

00:23:40.090 --> 00:23:43.000
we use a lot of the
tools like TensorFlow,

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:44.980
and a variety of some
other open source.

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:49.180
And I think as we
move ahead, we're

00:23:49.180 --> 00:23:52.420
going to use way more of
the machine learning tools

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:53.620
at Google.

00:23:53.620 --> 00:23:59.080
And in fact, I think this might
be a nice segue to the story

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:00.732
behind us moving into GCP.

00:24:00.732 --> 00:24:02.940
One of the reasons we are
excited about Google Cloud,

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:05.830
we made the transition
to Google Cloud,

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:08.770
is having been at
Google, I know firsthand

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:10.730
what a fantastic
infrastructure they have,

00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:13.057
but also that they are
some of the leaders.

00:24:13.057 --> 00:24:14.890
They have some of the
best machine learning.

00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:18.520
They're by far, I think,
the best machine learning

00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:19.960
technology in the world.

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:21.460
And we want to have
a piece of that.

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:22.876
We are a machine
learning company.

00:24:22.876 --> 00:24:26.470
We don't want to
compromise on that.

00:24:26.470 --> 00:24:29.170
And so we absolutely believe--

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:32.080
and we're very excited to try
some of the new hardware, some

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:34.720
of the new open source,
some of the new services

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:37.540
that Google Cloud is releasing.

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:39.040
NICK ORLOVE: It's
always interesting

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:40.880
hearing the
advancements that Google

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:43.300
is coming out with, specifically
around machine learning,

00:24:43.300 --> 00:24:44.820
big data.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:46.820
Cloud Next, which
happened recently,

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:48.970
I feel like maybe a quarter
of the announcements

00:24:48.970 --> 00:24:50.290
had machine learning
on it because it seems

00:24:50.290 --> 00:24:52.600
like machine learning is
coming to every single product

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:53.141
that we have.

00:24:53.141 --> 00:24:54.410
And it just makes sense.

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:56.350
So it's really interesting.

00:24:56.350 --> 00:24:58.450
I'm a little bit
curious about your story

00:24:58.450 --> 00:25:01.720
from migrating from another
cloud provider onto GCP.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:04.190
How was the migration, and
any kind of lessons learned?

00:25:06.129 --> 00:25:07.920
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE:
We started LiftIgniter

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:12.250
about four years
ago, and Google Cloud

00:25:12.250 --> 00:25:15.320
was in a much more
restricted form then.

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:22.410
A major provider was AWS, and
it was not a difficult choice

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:24.060
at that point.

00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:30.870
And then fast forward to
some time early last year.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:35.170
We were compelled by a
lot of the offerings.

00:25:35.170 --> 00:25:37.090
First of all, we
were very excited

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:39.730
that Google was taking the
cloud business seriously

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:42.500
because for a
variety of reasons,

00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:43.890
I think it makes a lot of sense.

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:45.640
They have some of the
best infrastructure.

00:25:45.640 --> 00:25:47.848
It's some insane infrastructure
internally at Google,

00:25:47.848 --> 00:25:50.300
and making that available
to the rest of the world

00:25:50.300 --> 00:25:53.540
is an idea that excites
me, appeals to me.

00:25:53.540 --> 00:25:55.990
And to some extent, LiftIgniter
had that same spirit

00:25:55.990 --> 00:25:57.550
of taking--

00:25:57.550 --> 00:26:04.050
and so we were compelled
to take a very close look.

00:26:04.050 --> 00:26:06.529
And there were a few reasons
that convinced us that

00:26:06.529 --> 00:26:09.070
the migration-- even though the
migration is going to be very

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:11.530
painful for startups--

00:26:11.530 --> 00:26:12.920
it still makes sense.

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:16.930
And that's a big decision coming
from a series A-level company

00:26:16.930 --> 00:26:17.710
to make.

00:26:17.710 --> 00:26:19.600
And that's the main
thing [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:21.850
bet on machine learning,
and that Google

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:24.190
is going to remain the
leader, and they're

00:26:24.190 --> 00:26:27.820
going to expose some of the
best machine learning technology

00:26:27.820 --> 00:26:29.230
on the cloud.

00:26:29.230 --> 00:26:33.220
The second thing
was we were very

00:26:33.220 --> 00:26:35.080
impressed by the people
we interacted with

00:26:35.080 --> 00:26:38.110
and their commitment
to helping startups.

00:26:38.110 --> 00:26:40.480
And startups need money.

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:43.030
Startups need great access
to great technology.

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:44.710
But another thing
they really would like

00:26:44.710 --> 00:26:47.680
is marketing exposure.

00:26:47.680 --> 00:26:51.430
And it's events like
this, for instance.

00:26:51.430 --> 00:26:53.590
Google has been phenomenal.

00:26:53.590 --> 00:26:56.800
We've had a ton of
exposure through blogs,

00:26:56.800 --> 00:27:00.740
through conferences,
through events like this

00:27:00.740 --> 00:27:03.420
at getting our message out.

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:05.170
So that was very, very
compelling for us.

00:27:05.170 --> 00:27:07.900
That was something we
were willing to take

00:27:07.900 --> 00:27:10.180
a hit on engineering
productivity for a few months

00:27:10.180 --> 00:27:11.800
during the migration.

00:27:11.800 --> 00:27:13.870
There were a few other
business reasons,

00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:16.690
like a lot of our customers
our e-commerce customers.

00:27:16.690 --> 00:27:19.140
And they're not
really comfortable

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:25.420
being vendors that are
completely hosted on AWS,

00:27:25.420 --> 00:27:27.110
for obvious reasons.

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:29.050
And then a few other
things, like obviously,

00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:32.280
infrastructures on certain
pieces was more exciting.

00:27:32.280 --> 00:27:35.750
The pricing was
friendlier in many ways.

00:27:35.750 --> 00:27:39.520
And so all of these reasons
together, we just made a call.

00:27:39.520 --> 00:27:42.160
And I think we were very
glad that we actually

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:43.464
made that decision.

00:27:43.464 --> 00:27:45.380
NICK ORLOVE: Well, hats
off to Jason and team.

00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:47.380
That startup program
is phenomenal, working

00:27:47.380 --> 00:27:49.520
with customers and everything.

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:51.430
So that's very awesome.

00:27:51.430 --> 00:27:54.140
Can you share some of the
things that LiftIgniter

00:27:54.140 --> 00:27:56.420
is going to be coming
out with in the future?

00:27:56.420 --> 00:27:57.961
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE:
Yes, absolutely.

00:27:57.961 --> 00:28:00.400
So when we initially started--

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:05.470
I'm a machine learning
PhD in my background.

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:08.710
It was all about machine, or
let's make the best algorithms.

00:28:08.710 --> 00:28:12.230
What we realized is, there are
limitations of the algorithm.

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:15.340
There are things that a
human does much better.

00:28:15.340 --> 00:28:17.657
And in order to build
a full-fledged product,

00:28:17.657 --> 00:28:19.490
we need to kind of
balance those two things.

00:28:19.490 --> 00:28:23.096
So we have this concept
of augmented intelligence.

00:28:23.096 --> 00:28:24.970
So we have these machine
learning algorithms,

00:28:24.970 --> 00:28:27.610
but working for you--

00:28:27.610 --> 00:28:33.400
working as tools that marketers
can use, in the same way

00:28:33.400 --> 00:28:36.010
that in Google Cloud is
making tools available rather

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:39.780
than solutions to
replace human beings.

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:42.040
So that is our focus,
and we're coming up

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:44.890
with a lot of tools
for technical,

00:28:44.890 --> 00:28:47.440
as well as non-technical
people, to leverage

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:50.920
the sophisticated
machine learning.

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:54.010
Use the insights from
them, use them as tools

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:56.020
to achieve their ends.

00:28:56.020 --> 00:28:58.270
So that's one of the
most exciting things

00:28:58.270 --> 00:28:59.575
we have on our roadmap.

00:28:59.575 --> 00:29:00.616
NICK ORLOVE: That's cool.

00:29:00.616 --> 00:29:01.540
Yeah, that's awesome.

00:29:01.540 --> 00:29:03.910
And then last question
for you before we

00:29:03.910 --> 00:29:06.820
jump into some of the
Q&amp;A. How do people

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:08.070
get in touch with LiftIgniter?

00:29:08.070 --> 00:29:11.320
I've heard that you're looking
for maybe some new people

00:29:11.320 --> 00:29:13.750
to join the team, and obviously
looking for customers.

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:18.040
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: Yeah, so
if you want to test the product,

00:29:18.040 --> 00:29:20.350
just send us an email
at info@liftigniter.com.

00:29:20.350 --> 00:29:22.160
We will get on a call
with you right away.

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:24.980
We have a lot of information
on the website as well.

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:28.060
And we're, obviously, looking
for amazing machine learning,

00:29:28.060 --> 00:29:31.750
back end, front end engineers,
marketing, as well as

00:29:31.750 --> 00:29:35.110
non-technical folks on the
sales and marketing site.

00:29:35.110 --> 00:29:37.690
So just drop us a note
at jobs@liftigniter.com,

00:29:37.690 --> 00:29:39.352
and we'll get back to you.

00:29:39.352 --> 00:29:40.810
NICK ORLOVE: Well,
Indraneel, thank

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:42.830
you so much for spending
the time with us.

00:29:42.830 --> 00:29:44.800
We're going to take
a quick break for all

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:46.010
of the questions that
have been submitted,

00:29:46.010 --> 00:29:47.732
so we can compile
them into one slide.

00:29:47.732 --> 00:29:49.690
And then we're going to
have you and Jason back

00:29:49.690 --> 00:29:51.580
to go over some
of those answers.

00:29:51.580 --> 00:29:52.810
So they can very much.

00:29:52.810 --> 00:29:53.440
Appreciate it.

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:55.190
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE:
Thanks so much, Nick.

00:31:05.795 --> 00:31:07.170
JASON SCOTT: All
right, everyone.

00:31:07.170 --> 00:31:08.110
Welcome back.

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:10.936
Again, Jason Scott, startup
program lead on the West Coast

00:31:10.936 --> 00:31:11.810
here at Google Cloud.

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:14.372
And we have Indraneel here,
founder of LiftIgniter.

00:31:14.372 --> 00:31:15.830
And we're back for
a few questions.

00:31:15.830 --> 00:31:18.140
So you want to just kick it off?

00:31:18.140 --> 00:31:18.450
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE:
Sure, absolutely.

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:19.325
JASON SCOTT: Awesome.

00:31:19.325 --> 00:31:20.570
So first question we have.

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:22.774
So now that LiftIgniter has
been on GCP for a while,

00:31:22.774 --> 00:31:24.190
are there some
things you wish you

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:26.046
had known from the beginning?

00:31:26.046 --> 00:31:28.170
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:31:28.170 --> 00:31:32.690
I think, definitely-- we
received a lot of help

00:31:32.690 --> 00:31:34.790
from Google team
while migrating,

00:31:34.790 --> 00:31:39.020
but there were a few things we
just could have planned better

00:31:39.020 --> 00:31:39.790
during migration.

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:43.690
So anyone who is planning
to migrate from, let's

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:45.710
say AWS or some
other cloud provider,

00:31:45.710 --> 00:31:51.800
to GCP, it's good to
probably dive down

00:31:51.800 --> 00:31:54.810
into some of the technologies,
which may look similar.

00:31:54.810 --> 00:31:58.670
For instance, [INAUDIBLE],,
which is a message queue on AWS

00:31:58.670 --> 00:32:01.970
versus Google Cloud Pub/Sub,
there are some subtle

00:32:01.970 --> 00:32:08.110
differences that can change
how much money it cost you,

00:32:08.110 --> 00:32:10.040
change how much--

00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:13.310
what kind of throughput
you're achieving.

00:32:13.310 --> 00:32:16.460
So both on the technical,
as well as expense side,

00:32:16.460 --> 00:32:20.090
you might want to redo
your code in a certain way

00:32:20.090 --> 00:32:23.540
in order to best use the
new technology that you

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:24.530
are migrating to.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:28.500
So some of the things
we thought were

00:32:28.500 --> 00:32:30.650
straightforward
may not be, so it's

00:32:30.650 --> 00:32:36.080
good to take a closer look
while doing the migration.

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:38.960
That was the theme
behind several pieces.

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:42.050
For instance, the
streaming jobs as well.

00:32:42.050 --> 00:32:46.100
When we went from streaming
jobs that were mostly self--

00:32:46.100 --> 00:32:49.450
it was managed by us
to Cloud Dataflow,

00:32:49.450 --> 00:32:51.950
there were some differences,
which were a pleasant surprise.

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:55.190
And there were some others
which were unexpected,

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:56.690
and we have to kind of maneuver.

00:32:56.690 --> 00:32:59.640
But if we had just known a
few more details in advance,

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:02.840
it would have helped with
a smoother transition.

00:33:02.840 --> 00:33:05.327
JASON SCOTT: Well, you made
it, which is also great.

00:33:05.327 --> 00:33:07.160
Second question that
came from the audience.

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:08.930
Any words of wisdom, I
guess aside from the one

00:33:08.930 --> 00:33:11.180
that you just mentioned,
for other aspiring startups

00:33:11.180 --> 00:33:12.935
and founders out there?

00:33:12.935 --> 00:33:15.835
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE: Yeah, there
is a lot of wisdom out there.

00:33:15.835 --> 00:33:16.460
I don't think--

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:21.590
I would strongly
recommend looking

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:23.060
at Y Combinator's content.

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:24.680
We actually went
through Y Combinator.

00:33:24.680 --> 00:33:28.190
And if you are a
first time founder,

00:33:28.190 --> 00:33:30.320
I would say that's one
of the best places.

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:32.530
You look up their
startup school advice,

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:36.110
look at their course
loads, and I would strongly

00:33:36.110 --> 00:33:39.230
recommend going through
that program as well.

00:33:39.230 --> 00:33:43.370
But beyond that, startups
are exhilarating.

00:33:43.370 --> 00:33:48.800
It's like you're going to live
life in a more intense way.

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:53.850
So brace for it, but its
going to be worth it.

00:33:53.850 --> 00:33:56.090
JASON SCOTT: Third, and you
mentioned the challenges

00:33:56.090 --> 00:33:58.070
of migration already, but
what are some of the benefits

00:33:58.070 --> 00:34:00.490
and challenges-- some of the
other benefits and challenges

00:34:00.490 --> 00:34:05.260
that LiftIgniter faced on
GCP throughout your journey?

00:34:05.260 --> 00:34:07.010
INDRANEEL MUKHERJEE:
The benefits, again--

00:34:07.010 --> 00:34:09.260
I kind of touched
upon these issues,

00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:11.510
like the pricing
is much friendlier.

00:34:11.510 --> 00:34:16.956
It helps us reduce planning
and some extra overhead.

00:34:16.956 --> 00:34:18.830
It's easier to sell to
some of our customers,

00:34:18.830 --> 00:34:21.469
again, the e-commerce customers.

00:34:21.469 --> 00:34:23.840
Some of the tools are
just a little bit more

00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:24.820
developer-friendly.

00:34:24.820 --> 00:34:27.889
I keep hearing this from
a lot of our developers,

00:34:27.889 --> 00:34:33.185
like the permission system
is a little more friendly.

00:34:36.110 --> 00:34:37.850
Google Cloud
Pub/Sub, it has a lot

00:34:37.850 --> 00:34:41.929
of-- and Cloud Dataflow,
they are very well-designed

00:34:41.929 --> 00:34:46.104
services, which reduces
the coding complexities.

00:34:46.104 --> 00:34:48.020
There have been like
innumerable little things

00:34:48.020 --> 00:34:50.880
like that, which we really like.

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:53.239
And then, of course,
some big things

00:34:53.239 --> 00:34:59.286
like BigQuery, which is a
phenomenal piece of product,

00:34:59.286 --> 00:34:59.910
infrastructure.

00:34:59.910 --> 00:35:03.980
It has simplified a lot of our
product, and it has cut down

00:35:03.980 --> 00:35:06.110
are dev time by several months.

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:08.390
Totally worth the price.

00:35:08.390 --> 00:35:12.570
And some of the
autoscaling logic,

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:16.770
et cetera, which has also
worked pretty well for us.

00:35:16.770 --> 00:35:18.500
And so that was some
of the benefits.

00:35:18.500 --> 00:35:22.430
The challenges, again,
some more better research

00:35:22.430 --> 00:35:25.820
before doing the migration could
have helped us create a better

00:35:25.820 --> 00:35:27.980
plan for migration.

00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:34.730
And then the other thing is,
Google Cloud has some fantastic

00:35:34.730 --> 00:35:37.730
features, but there
are some rough edges.

00:35:37.730 --> 00:35:40.450
Not many, but some, like for
instance, a load balancer

00:35:40.450 --> 00:35:42.770
ran down a few weeks ago.

00:35:42.770 --> 00:35:50.540
And then there were some
minor bits and pieces.

00:35:50.540 --> 00:35:53.440
There was just a little
bit more monitoring--

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:56.060
less monitoring metrics
that are available,

00:35:56.060 --> 00:35:57.770
which is kind of
important for us

00:35:57.770 --> 00:36:00.200
because we have strong
SLAs with our customers.

00:36:00.200 --> 00:36:02.320
Again, nothing-- these
aren't showstoppers.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:06.840
These are all things
we can work around.

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:09.130
And I'm sure these will
improve very quickly,

00:36:09.130 --> 00:36:12.280
given the pace at which
Google Cloud has improved

00:36:12.280 --> 00:36:14.840
over the last few years.

00:36:14.840 --> 00:36:18.100
JASON SCOTT: Well, thank
you for being candid.

00:36:18.100 --> 00:36:20.340
I guess the last question
is one that I can take.

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:23.740
It says, if I'm a startup, how
do I get engaged with Google?

00:36:23.740 --> 00:36:26.050
So I think with
that, I would love

00:36:26.050 --> 00:36:28.660
to let everyone know
that as a viewer

00:36:28.660 --> 00:36:31.120
or as a listener
to this, you're all

00:36:31.120 --> 00:36:34.450
eligible to join our
startup program at our start

00:36:34.450 --> 00:36:38.140
tier, which is kind of our early
stage tier for our program.

00:36:38.140 --> 00:36:41.590
That includes $3,000 in Cloud
credits and Firebase credits,

00:36:41.590 --> 00:36:43.160
as well as a lot
of other benefits--

00:36:43.160 --> 00:36:48.080
G Suite, if you're on that, as
well as the technical support

00:36:48.080 --> 00:36:51.220
that we mentioned
earlier as well.

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:53.830
With that, I would
like everyone to stay

00:36:53.830 --> 00:36:58.090
tuned for our next session,
which is Google Sheets--

00:36:58.090 --> 00:37:00.310
tips and tricks to
boost productivity.

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:02.590
Something I definitely
will also tune into.

00:37:02.590 --> 00:37:04.500
Coming to you live
from New York.

